---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import HeroJourney from '../components/HeroJourney.astro';
import Examples from '../components/Examples.astro';
import ProviderStrip from '../components/ProviderStrip.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import brandLogo from '../assets/logo_dumbdiagrams_highres.png';
import websitePackage from '../../package.json';
import { url, npmUrl, repository } from '../lib/site';
const releaseVersion = websitePackage.version;
const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Dumb Diagrams', applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Windows, macOS, Linux', runtimePlatform: 'Node.js 24+',
  description: 'Open-source architecture diagrams as code for AI coding agents and developers. Portable .dgm source, deterministic rendering, and client-side diagram processing.',
  license: 'https://spdx.org/licenses/AGPL-3.0-only.html',
  isAccessibleForFree: true, downloadUrl: npmUrl,
  ...(Astro.site ? { url: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href } : {}),
  ...(repository ? { sameAs: repository } : {}),
};
---
<Base title="Architecture Diagrams as Code for AI Agents" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow"><i></i> LLM-FIRST ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS</span><span class="version mono">OPEN SOURCE / v{releaseVersion}</span></div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <h1 id="hero-title">A little opinionated.<br /><span>A lot clearer<span class="hero-period">.</span></span></h1>
          <div class="hero-description"><p>Architecture diagrams as code for AI coding agents. Let an LLM write reviewable source, then refine the result on your own machine.</p></div>
          <div class="hero-start"><Install /></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-terminal"><HeroJourney /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ProviderStrip />

    <Examples />

    <section class="manifesto section" data-reveal aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
      <div class="manifesto-main"><span class="eyebrow">02 / LESS GUESSWORK, MORE ENGINE</span><h2 id="manifesto-title">Describe the architecture.<br /><em>Let the engine place it.</em></h2><p>Give the model the architecture rather than a canvas full of coordinates. The engine handles placement and connector routes, producing a repeatable first render that you can adjust.</p><a class="button light" href={url('docs/routing/')}>Inside the engine <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="manifesto-notes"><article><span class="mono">01 / DETERMINISTIC ROUTING</span><h3>The same setup produces a repeatable render.</h3><p>The document, engine and catalog versions, and saved view determine the output. Routes are scored for obstacles, crossings, bends, and distance, with A* search when simple paths are blocked. Dense cases can still need review; warnings flag where the result may need attention.</p></article><article><span class="mono">02 / YOUR FINAL SAY</span><h3>You can still adjust the result.</h3><p>Move a service, choose a port, or draw a manual route in Studio. Your positions and visual overrides stay in the <code>.dgm</code> file, so the next render preserves your decisions.</p></article><article><span class="mono">03 / PRIVATE BY DESIGN</span><h3>Your diagrams. Yours alone.</h3><p>Local Studio saves files on your device, and the browser editor keeps work in browser storage. We commit that Dumb Diagrams servers will never receive or store your diagrams, including in the planned in-browser Studio. <a href={url('privacy/')}>Read the privacy details.</a></p></article><article><span class="mono">04 / LLM-FIRST, TOKEN-CONSCIOUS</span><h3>Use tokens for the architecture.</h3><p>Ask an agent to write compact source instead of issuing repeated shape-by-shape MCP tool calls. Letting the engine handle layout can reduce token use and make lighter models practical. Savings depend on the model, context, and workflow.</p></article></div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq section" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / PRACTICAL QUESTIONS</span><h2 id="faq-title">What to expect.</h2><p>A few things to know before you start.</p><div class="faq-brand" aria-hidden="true"><Image src={brandLogo} width={440} widths={[440, 880, 1320]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 324px, 440px" quality={95} format="webp" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div></div><div class="faq-list">
      <details><summary>Why use an AI agent to create architecture diagrams?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Describe your system in ordinary language and let a coding agent draft its readable <code>.dgm</code> source. Dumb Diagrams validates the file and handles layout and routing, so the agent can focus on your architecture instead of placing every box and arrow by hand. Review the changes in Git, then refine the result in Studio. You can still write the source yourself, and no AI model is built in. <a href={url('docs/agent-workflow/')}>See how the workflow works.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need a coding agent?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes, in practice. We recommend using a coding agent. You can write <code>.dgm</code> by hand, but an agent is the practical way to create and revise diagrams. Use any capable model, including open-source models; most of our testing so far uses lower-cost frontier models from the GPT and Claude families. Run <code>dumbdiagrams init</code> where you want the project, then give the agent the generated <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>.dumbdiagrams/</code> instructions. Refine the result in Studio.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Where do my diagrams live, and are they sent anywhere?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Your diagrams stay on your device. Local Studio opens and saves portable <code>.dgm</code> files on your machine; the standalone editor keeps work in browser storage until you download it. We commit that Dumb Diagrams servers will never receive or store your diagrams. The same promise applies to the planned in-browser Studio: diagram processing stays on your device. If you choose to share diagram context with an outside coding agent, that provider’s own settings and terms apply. <a href={url('docs/studio/')}>Read about Studio.</a> <a href={url('privacy/')}>See our privacy commitment.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use it for clouds other than AWS?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. AWS was the starting point, and the catalog now includes Azure, Google Cloud, and cloud-agnostic assets too. Support for those may be less polished than the AWS workflow. If something you need for your provider is missing, contributions are welcome{repository ? <>: see <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a></> : null}.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is this different from a whiteboard?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>You describe the services, boundaries, and connections, and Dumb Diagrams lays them out for you. You can then refine the result. The engine applies layout, icon alignment, and routing rules to turn that source into a diagram. Complex layouts and crowded routes can still need review. An agent may give you a different draft each time; rendering the same <code>.dgm</code> source is repeatable when you use matching engine and catalog versions and keep the saved layout the same. The layout rules mostly follow AWS architecture guidance, with a few design choices of our own. It’s built for architecture diagrams, so it won’t cover general illustration, track your live infrastructure, or give you a hosted workspace for editing together.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is it free and open source?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. Dumb Diagrams is released under AGPL-3.0-only. The provider icons come with their own terms; see the <a href={url('LICENSE.txt')}>project license</a> and <a href={url('ICON-NOTICES.txt')}>icon notices</a>. We want to build responsible, privacy-respecting software, and welcome contributions to the code, documentation, tests, and issue discussions{repository ? <>. <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>Find out how to contribute.</a></> : '.'}</p></details>
    </div></section>

    <section class="closing" data-reveal><div><span class="eyebrow">START WITH ONE DIAGRAM</span><h2>Put the architecture<br /><em>on the page.</em></h2></div><a href={url('docs/quick-start/')} class="closing-cta trace-border" data-trace><span class="trace-orbit" aria-hidden="true"><span class="trace-orbit__light"></span></span>Create your first diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </main>
</Base>
